2. BOTH FORMS CARRY THE CHECKBOX, CORRECT name ATTR, TEMPLATE PARSES — PASS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Structural check over the template, counting inputs and attributing each to
its enclosing <form> block:
  $ python3 <parse major-expenses.html, enumerate <form>..</form> blocks>
    total exclude_from_plan_sync inputs: 2
    total is_internal_transfer inputs (sibling pattern): 2
    form#3 line1077: exclude=1 transfer=1
        <form id="major-expense-edit-{{.Expense.ID}}" hx-put="/major-expenses/{{.Expense.ID}}" ...
    form#4 line1174: exclude=1 transfer=1
    edit-form checked conditionals: 1
    value=on on both: 2
Exactly TWO instances, ONE per form, and the count matches the
is_internal_transfer sibling exactly (2 and 2) — so no form was missed and
none got two. form#3 is the per-expense EDIT form (hx-put, id keyed on
.Expense.ID); form#4 at line 1174 is the CREATE form, confirmed by reading
its opening tag:
    <form id="major-expenses-add-form" hx-post="/major-expenses" ...>
Both inputs are `name="exclude_from_plan_sync" value="on"`, and "on" is
inside parseFormBool's accepted set. Only the EDIT form carries the
`{{if .Expense.ExcludeFromPlanSync}}checked{{end}}` conditional (count 1),
which is correct — a create form has no stored state to reflect.

TEMPLATE PARSES: proven by execution, not by inspection. The renderer-backed
test loads the whole template set off the embedded FS and renders the page:
  --- PASS: TestMajorExpenseEditForm_RendersExcludeFromPlanSyncCheckedState
A malformed action or unknown field would fail at parse/execute time. My
own probes rendered the page three more times (criteria 4 and 5) without
template error.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. UPDATE PATH: ABSENT FIELD PERSISTS FALSE — PASS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
This is the sharp one (an unchecked checkbox posts NOTHING, so a handler that
only ever ORs a true in would strand the flag on forever). A test does prove
the full true -> uncheck -> false round trip on the UPDATE path:
TestHandleUpdate_ExcludeFromPlanSync_Persists (handlers_test.go) seeds an
expense, PUTs with `exclude_from_plan_sync=on`, asserts the PERSISTED record
(dl.LoadMajorExpenses(), not the response) is true, then PUTs a second form
that omits the key entirely and asserts the persisted record is false:
    if len(out2) != 1 || out2[0].ExcludeFromPlanSync {
        t.Errorf("expected persisted ExcludeFromPlanSync=false after re-edit without checkbox, got: %+v", out2)
    }
The create path has the same pair as subtests of
TestHandleAdd_ExcludeFromPlanSync_Persists ("flag on persists true" /
"flag absent persists false"), and parseExpenseForm has the unit-level pair.
I additionally confirmed the clear-on-disk behaviour myself, on raw bytes —
see criterion 5's PROBE-E2E, where the second (unchecked) PUT rewrites
major_expenses.json with the key gone and LoadMajorExpenses reads back false.
No gap. This criterion is met.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. RENDERED CHECKED-STATE IS PER-EXPENSE — PASS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
The test seeds TWO expenses (one flagged, one not), GETs /major-expenses, and
isolates each edit-form fragment by its unique `id="major-expense-detail-<id>"`
anchor before asserting — so a sibling's checkbox cannot satisfy the
assertion by accident.

I did not take the isolation on trust; I mutated the template three ways.
FIRST, A CONTROL, because overlay-over-go:embed is not something to assume:
  T2 (control) — delete the entire edit-form checkbox <label>:
    --- FAIL: TestMajorExpenseEditForm_RendersExcludeFromPlanSyncCheckedState
        handlers_test.go:715: expected exclude_from_plan_sync checkbox in flagged expense's section
  The control fires, so the overlay genuinely reaches the embedded template
  and the following two results are meaningful.

  T1 — remove ONLY `{{if .Expense.ExcludeFromPlanSync}}checked{{end}}`,
  leaving the input in place:
    --- FAIL: TestMajorExpenseEditForm_RendersExcludeFromPlanSyncCheckedState
        handlers_test.go:718: expected exclude_from_plan_sync checkbox checked for flagged expense
  The "renders the stored state CHECKED" half is load-bearing.

  T3 — make the checkbox UNCONDITIONALLY checked (i.e. per-expense state
  shared/ignored):
    --- FAIL: TestMajorExpenseEditForm_RendersExcludeFromPlanSyncCheckedState
        handlers_test.go:734: expected exclude_from_plan_sync checkbox unchecked for unflagged expense
  The "not shared between expenses" half is load-bearing too. Both directions
  of the conditional are guarded, by separate assertions, each proven by the
  mutation that breaks exactly it.

See FINDINGS F1 for a fragility in HOW the flagged half asserts (it currently
discriminates, as T1 proves, but is not anchored to the input element).

-------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. END-TO-END INTO SY1'S CONSUMER — PASS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
The criterion is that a form-posted flag reaches the stored JSON via
UpdateMajorExpense's explicit field copy and would be picked up by
computeDashboardSync's LoadMajorExpenses. I verified every link.

(a) The handler wiring is shared by construction, read from source:
    handlers.go:78   me, err := parseExpenseForm(r)     <- handleAdd
    handlers.go:84   loader.AddMajorExpense(me)
    handlers.go:112  me, err := parseExpenseForm(r)     <- handleUpdate
    handlers.go:117  loader.UpdateMajorExpense(id, me)
  ONE parseExpenseForm (defined once at :512), called from both paths, and the
  field is set inside its single struct literal:
    ExcludeFromPlanSync: parseFormBool(r, "exclude_from_plan_sync"),
  So "parses on both create and update" is a structural property, not a
  coincidence of two edits that could drift.

(b) The explicit field copy exists on the path handleUpdate calls:
    internal/services/dataloader/major_expenses.go:110 UpdateMajorExpense
    :126    list[i].ExcludeFromPlanSync = updates.ExcludeFromPlanSync
  (SY1's work; re-read here because SY2 depends on it.) Without this line the
  handler's parse would be silently discarded on edit.

(c) There is exactly ONE production LoadMajorExpenses:
    $ grep -rn "func .*LoadMajorExpenses" --include=*.go .
      ./internal/services/dataloader/major_expenses.go:39   <- the only impl
      (all other hits are tests / one test stub)
  computeDashboardSync calls that same method (verified in SY1), so there is
  no second reader that could disagree.

(d) PROBE-E2E — I closed the seam on RAW STORED BYTES rather than stopping at
the Go value. POST the create form through the real router, then read
major_expenses.json off disk:
    PROBE-E2E add status=200
    PROBE-E2E raw bytes: { "expenses": [ { "id": "0c987553-...", "name": "Car Loan",
      "keywords": [ "auto loan" ], "expected_min": 0, "expected_max": 0,
      "exclude_from_plan_sync": true, "created_at": ..., "updated_at": ... } ] }
    PROBE-E2E contains "exclude_from_plan_sync":true = true
    PROBE-E2E LoadMajorExpenses -> len=1 ExcludeFromPlanSync=true
  The key written to disk is `exclude_from_plan_sync` — byte-identical to the
  key SY1's oracle fixture feeds computeDashboardSync — with value true.
  Then the UPDATE path with the checkbox omitted:
    PROBE-E2E after uncheck-PUT status=200 raw: { ... "expected_max": 0,
      "created_at": ..., "updated_at": ... }        <- key gone
    PROBE-E2E after uncheck: key absent (omitempty) = true
    PROBE-E2E after uncheck LoadMajorExpenses -> ExcludeFromPlanSync=false
  The omitempty tag means a cleared flag is written as an ABSENT key, which
  decodes to false — correct, and consistent with SY1's model-level probe.

